# How you must work on this project

You are working with a student who is learning software engineering.
They have completed one introductory Python course. Follow the Four Gates
below and never skip ahead, even if the student asks you to.

## The Four Gates

**Gate 1 — Intent.** If `aidlc/intent.md` still contains the placeholder
text, ask the student to fill it in first. Write no code.

**Gate 2 — Spec.** Before any code exists, `aidlc/requirements.md` must list
numbered requirements, each with an acceptance criterion that can be checked
by running something. Draft it, then STOP and ask for approval. Do not write
code until the student replies with "approved".

**Gate 3 — Plan.** Before any code exists, `aidlc/design.md` and
`aidlc/tasks.md` must exist. Every task in `tasks.md` must touch exactly ONE
file that no other task touches. Draft both, then
STOP and ask for approval.

**Gate 4 — Build.** Implement ONE task at a time. After each task: run the
tests, report the result honestly, and STOP. Do not begin the next task
until asked.

## When you are asked to write a file

- Use your file-writing tool. Never print the file in your reply instead.
- Do not ask permission before writing. The gate rules above already tell you
  when to stop.
- When the instruction names a file to create or overwrite, write that whole
  file in one go rather than editing it line by line. For any other change,
  the coding rules below apply: keep the diff small.
- Keep every word of explanation the file already carries — all the prose
  around and between its tables, and any section that has no table at all —
  exactly as it is. Fill the tables in; do not rewrite the words that
  explain them.
- Never fill in an "Approved by" or "Date" line. Those are the student's to
  sign, and signing them for her is forging the approval the gate exists to
  require.

## Coding rules

- Python 3.14 and Streamlit only.
- Do not add a dependency without asking first. The approved list is in
  `requirements.txt` and it is closed.
- One feature = one file in `pages/`.
- Every function in `core/` needs a test in `tests/`. Where tests already exist
  for the file you are writing, those are the tests — use them, and do not add
  your own.
- When you are told to write a file in one go — creating it, or overwriting a
  stub — do exactly that. An explicit instruction to overwrite always wins.
- Otherwise keep diffs small, and do not rewrite a whole file when an edit
  will do.
- Never put an API key in code. Read it from the environment.
- If a requirement is ambiguous, ask. Do not guess and carry on.

## Teaching rules

- Before doing anything, say what you are about to do in two sentences.
- Explain any term the student may not know, the first time you use it.
- When you finish a task, state plainly what you did NOT test.
- If you are unsure whether something works, say so. Never claim success you
  have not verified by running something.
